<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The storage contracts, as something a customer can reference.

    They lived in Boltway.Storage.Tests, which was `IsPackable=true` with the right reason written
    on it — a store contract nobody outside this repository can run is a contract only this
    repository is held to. What it published was not usable, and it is the same three defects
    Boltway.Interaction.Testing was split out to fix, measured again on the packed output:

      * dependencies included Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, xunit.runner.visualstudio and
        coverlet.collector, so referencing the contract dragged a test *runner* and a coverage
        collector into the customer's project;
      * the assembly shipped a runtimeconfig.json, because it was packaged as an executable test
        project rather than as a library;
      * and it carried this repository's own seven InMemory*Tests classes, so a customer running
        `dotnet test` ran Boltway's tests inside their own suite and read the results as being
        about their store.

    The fix was written once and applied to the interaction half only. This is the storage half,
    and it is the half that reached nuget.org: Boltway.Storage.Tests 0.1.0 is on the feed with all
    three defects in it, and nuget.org has no delete. That package is superseded by this one rather
    than replaced — the id stays taken.

    So: the abstract contracts live here, with xunit and nothing else. The derivations stay next
    door, where they are this repository's tests rather than anybody's contract.

    No test runner here on purpose. This assembly is never executed by itself — a customer's own
    test project supplies the runner, and adding one would put a second copy in their build.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>

    <!--
      No package validation while the baseline is 0.1.0, because there is nothing to validate
      against. Every other id here has a 0.1.0 on nuget.org that a consumer may already have
      restored, and Directory.Build.props makes that the ApiCompat baseline; this id was first
      published at 0.2.0, so asking for a 0.1.0 of it is a restore failure rather than a check.

      **The condition is how this expires, and it is a condition rather than a note for a reason.**
      This said "remove this the release after the first one that ships it", which is an instruction
      to somebody who has to remember it — a year later, mid-release, in a file nobody opens unless
      they are changing the contracts. The moment the baseline moves off 0.1.0 it names a version
      this id actually has, and the opt-out stops applying by itself. Nothing to remember, and no
      release where the check is off for a reason that has already stopped being true.
    -->
    <EnablePackageValidation Condition="'$(PackageValidationBaselineVersion)' == '0.1.0'">false</EnablePackageValidation>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <Description>
      The IGrantStore, IUserStore, IClientStore, IConsentStore, IUserTokenStore, IAdminAuditStore
      and IClientAssertionReplayStore contracts, as xunit base classes. Derive from them, supply
      your implementation, and the suite that defines each seam runs against it.
    </Description>

    <!--
      CA1707 (no underscores in a public member name) off, CS1591 (missing XML comment) left on,
      and the split is the point — the same reasoning as Boltway.Interaction.Testing.

      The names are underscored because a contract method's name is what a customer reads in their
      own test runner when it fails: `Redeeming_many_times_in_parallel_still_succeeds_exactly_once`
      is the finding. The docs stay required because this is an API surface now.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The assertion library only. Not Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, not the runner, not coverlet:
         those belong to whatever project executes tests, which is never this one. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The seams only, and deliberately not Boltway.Storage.InMemory: a contract that referenced an
    implementation would hand every customer a second one they did not ask for, and the in-memory
    stores are what this repository's own derivations use, not what the contract is about.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Boltway.AuthorizationServer.Abstractions\Boltway.AuthorizationServer.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
